<body>

<div class="poster">

  <!-- ════════ HEADER ════════ -->
  <div class="header">
    <div class="header-top">
      <div class="nature-badge">NATURE 2024</div>
      <div class="header-meta">Landmark Study &middot; <span class="doi">DOI: 10.1038/s41586-024-07487-w</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="header-title">Mapping Neural Circuit Architecture:<br>Advances in Brain Connectivity Analysis</div>
    <div class="header-subtitle">A comprehensive study revealing new principles of neural circuit organization through large-scale connectomics and functional mapping</div>
  </div>

  <!-- ════════ METRICS BANNER ════════ -->
  <div class="metrics-banner">
    <div class="metric-card">
      <div class="metric-value">~125K</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Neurons Mapped</div>
      <div class="metric-desc">Reconstructed in the connectome dataset</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card">
      <div class="metric-value">~54M</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Synaptic Connections</div>
      <div class="metric-desc">Individual synapses identified &amp; catalogued</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card">
      <div class="metric-value">37</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Circuit Motifs</div>
      <div class="metric-desc">Distinct recurring connectivity motifs</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card">
      <div class="metric-value">94.2%</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Classification Accuracy</div>
      <div class="metric-desc">Automated neuron-type classification</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card">
      <div class="metric-value">2024</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Nature Publication</div>
      <div class="metric-desc">s41586-024-07487-w</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ════════ BACKGROUND ════════ -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-label blue">Background</div>
    <div class="section-title">Background &amp; Motivation</div>
    <div class="section-text">
      Understanding how the brain processes information requires detailed maps of neural connectivity. While classical neuroanatomy provided foundational insights, the field has long lacked the resolution and scale needed to capture complete circuit-level wiring diagrams. This study represents a landmark effort to reconstruct and analyze neural circuits at unprecedented scale, bridging the gap between structural connectivity and functional organization.
    </div>
    <div class="background-grid">
      <div class="bg-point">
        <div class="bg-point-title">Scale Gap</div>
        <div class="bg-point-text">Prior connectomics work mapped at most thousands of neurons — insufficient to capture circuit-level organization across cortical layers and cell types.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-point">
        <div class="bg-point-title">Technology Convergence</div>
        <div class="bg-point-text">Advances in electron microscopy, automated segmentation, and computational neuroscience have converged to make large-scale connectomics feasible.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-point">
        <div class="bg-point-title">Structure-Function Bridge</div>
        <div class="bg-point-text">Bridging structural connectivity and functional organization requires mapping at both the synaptic and circuit level simultaneously.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-point">
        <div class="bg-point-title">Open Resource</div>
        <div class="bg-point-text">Creating an open-access, comprehensive connectomic resource to accelerate discovery across the neuroscience community.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ════════ METHODOLOGY ════════ -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-label teal">Methods</div>
    <div class="section-title">Methodology &amp; Technical Approach</div>
    <div class="section-text">A multi-modal pipeline combining serial-section electron microscopy with advanced machine learning for automated neuron reconstruction.</div>
    <div class="methods-row">
      <div class="pipeline-viz">
        <div id="pipeline-chart"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="methods-table-wrap">
        <table class="methods-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Serial-section EM at 4 nm &times; 4 nm &times; 30 nm resolution</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Segmentation</td><td>Deep learning-based automated neuron tracing with human proofreading</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Synapse Detection</td><td>Convolutional neural network classifier (94.2% accuracy)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Circuit Analysis</td><td>Graph-theoretic motif detection and clustering algorithms</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Validation</td><td>Calcium imaging and optogenetic perturbation experiments</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ════════ KEY FINDINGS ════════ -->
  <div class="section findings-section">
    <div class="section-label gold">Key Findings</div>
    <div class="section-title">Principal Discoveries</div>
    <div class="findings-grid">
      <div class="finding-card">
        <div class="finding-number">Finding 01</div>
        <div class="finding-title">Modular Connectivity Motifs</div>
        <div class="finding-stat">37 distinct types</div>
        <div class="finding-desc">37 distinct connectivity motifs identified as recurring building blocks of larger circuits, suggesting a modular wiring logic — analogous to logic gates in digital circuits.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding-card">
        <div class="finding-number">Finding 02</div>
        <div class="finding-title">Hub Neuron Architecture</div>
        <div class="finding-stat">3.5% &rarr; 28% output</div>
        <div class="finding-desc">Hub neurons comprising only ~3.5% of the population accounted for over 28% of total synaptic output, indicating a highly non-uniform distribution of influence.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding-card">
        <div class="finding-number">Finding 03</div>
        <div class="finding-title">Layer-Specific Connectivity</div>
        <div class="finding-stat">4.7 : 1 ratio</div>
        <div class="finding-desc">Feedforward connections dominated between layers 2/3 &rarr; 4, while recurrent connections were enriched within layer 5 with a 4.7:1 recurrent-to-feedforward ratio.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding-card">
        <div class="finding-number">Finding 04</div>
        <div class="finding-title">Inhibitory Specificity</div>
        <div class="finding-stat">6.2&times; higher</div>
        <div class="finding-desc">Inhibitory interneurons formed connections with 6.2&times; higher specificity than excitatory neurons, challenging assumptions of broad inhibitory blanket coverage.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="findings-bottom">
      <div id="findings-chart"></div>
      <div class="findings-table-container">
        <table class="findings-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Quantitative Result</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Connectivity motifs</td><td>37 distinct types</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hub neuron fraction</td><td>3.5% &rarr; 28% output</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Layer 5 recurrent ratio</td><td>4.7 : 1</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Inhibitory specificity</td><td>6.2&times; vs excitatory</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Total neurons mapped</td><td>~125,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Total synapses catalogued</td><td>~54 million</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ════════ IMPLICATIONS ════════ -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-label coral">Implications</div>
    <div class="section-title">Implications &amp; Future Directions</div>
    <div class="implications-grid">
      <div class="implication-item">
        <div class="implication-icon">
          <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"/><path d="M12 1v4m0 14v4m-8.66-15l3.46 2m10.4 6l3.46 2m-17.32 0l3.46-2m10.4-6l3.46-2"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="implication-title">Modular Circuit Logic</div>
        <div class="implication-text">The brain employs a finite vocabulary of circuit elements, analogous to logic gates in digital circuits. This modular wiring logic simplifies the computational principles underlying cortical processing.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="implication-item">
        <div class="implication-icon">
          <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M22 12h-4l-3 9L9 3l-3 9H2"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="implication-title">Clinical Relevance</div>
        <div class="implication-text">Hub neurons as disproportionate drivers of network activity have direct implications for understanding how focal lesions or targeted stimulation can produce widespread effects in neurological disease.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="implication-item">
        <div class="implication-icon">
          <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M17 21v-2a4 4 0 00-4-4H5a4 4 0 00-4 4v2"/><circle cx="9" cy="7" r="4"/><path d="M23 21v-2a4 4 0 00-3-3.87M16 3.13a4 4 0 010 7.75"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="implication-title">BCI Design</div>
        <div class="implication-text">Identification of hub neuron connectivity patterns informs brain-computer interface design by revealing optimal stimulation and recording targets for next-generation neuroprosthetics.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="implications-future">
      Future work will extend this connectomic approach to additional brain regions and species, with the ultimate goal of building a complete wiring diagram of the mammalian brain. The <strong>open-access dataset</strong> and analysis tools released alongside this publication are expected to accelerate discovery across the neuroscience community.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ════════ FOOTER ════════ -->
  <div class="footer">
    Source: Nature (2024), DOI: 10.1038/s41586-024-07487-w &middot; Presented at Neuroscience Symposium 2024
  </div>

</div>

